# Network Architecture

## Requirements
- Application must survive an Availability Zone failure.
- Application servers must not be directly accessible from the internet.
- Database must not be publicly accessible.
- Public users must access the web application over HTTPS.

## VPC
CIDR: 10.0.0.0/16

## Subnet Design
| Subnet | Availability Zone | CIDR | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| Public A | AZ-A | 10.0.1.0/24 | Load balancer / NAT |
| Public B | AZ-B | 10.0.2.0/24 | Load balancer / NAT |
| App A | AZ-A | 10.0.11.0/24 | Application |
| App B | AZ-B | 10.0.12.0/24 | Application |
| DB A | AZ-A | 10.0.21.0/24 | Database |
| DB B | AZ-B | 10.0.22.0/24 | Database |

## Routing
### Public Subnet
Public A and Public B have a default route to the Internet Gateway.
0.0.0.0/0 -> Internet Gateway

### Private Application Subnets
App A:
0.0.0.0/0 -> NAT Gateway A

App B:
0.0.0.0/0 -> NAT Gateway B

Allow Applications to initiate outbound connection to the internet, without allowing access from the internet.

### Private Database Subnets
DB A and DB B do not have a default route to the internet.

Communication between the application and database tiers uses
the VPC local route.

## VPC Endpoints
### S3 Gateway Endpoint
The application tier uses an S3 Gateway VPC Endpoint for private
connectivity to Amazon S3.

S3 traffic does not need to traverse the NAT Gateway or public internet.

### Interface Endpoint
An Interface VPC Endpoint can be used for private connectivity to
supported AWS services such as AWS Secrets Manager.

This allows private application instances to communicate with the
service without relying on NAT-based internet connectivity.

## Security Groups
### ALB-SG
Inbound: Port HTTPS 443 from the internet
Purpose: Allows public HTTPS traffic to acess the App Load Balancer

### App-SG
Inbound: Application only allows traffic from ALB-SG
Purpose: Allows application traffic only from the Application Load Balancer and prevents 
direct inbound internet access to application instances.

### DB-SG
Inbound: PostgreSQL TCP 5432 from App-SG only allowed.
Purpose: Only allows DB connections from the app.

## Availability Strategy
The application is deployed across two Availability Zones to reduce dependency on a single AZ.

App Load Balancer distributes incoming traffic across healthy application targets.

Each Availability Zone has its own NAT Gateway, an app in one AZ does not depend on a NAT Gateway located in 
another AZ for outbound connectivity.

The database launched in multiple AZ supports the high-availability in case of failure.

## Architecture Decisions
### AD-001 — Multi-AZ Network
Decision: Launch application and db across multiple Availability Zones

Reason: This Architecture allows high-availability to keep the app as healthy as posible in case of one AZ failure.

Trade-off: Higher costs due to replication across AZs

### AD-002 — Private Application Tier
Decision: Deploy application instances in private subnets without public IP addresses

Reason: Users access the application through the public Application Load Balancer.
The application instances therefore do not require direct inbound internet access.

Trade-off: Private instances require another mechanism, such as NAT Gateways,
when outbound internet connectivity is required.

### AD-003 — Database Network Isolation
Decision: DB has no default internet rout

Reason: DB doesn't require internect connection

Trade-off: The database cannot initiate connections to public internet destinations. 
Additional private connectivity or routing would need to be introduced if future database 
requirements require access to external services.

## Alternatives Considered
### Single NAT Gateway
A single NAT Gateway could be used to reduce infrastructure cost.

However, this would create a cross-AZ dependency. If the Availability Zone containing the NAT 
Gateway becomes unavailable, application instances in the other AZ could lose their outbound 
internet path.

For a development environment where cost is more important than
high availability, a single NAT Gateway could be considered.
